# Build context exclusions.
#
# Two of these are not about speed:
#
# `data/` holds the runtime SQLite database, the Fernet key, the bootstrap token and
# uploaded files. Docker copies the whole context to the daemon before the build starts,
# and anything COPYed lands in an image layer — so leaving it in means a key file can be
# recovered from a layer even if a later stage deletes it.
#
# `.venv/` and `node_modules/` are hundreds of megabytes that would be transferred on
# every build and then never used: the image installs its own dependencies.
#
# `config/` is NOT excluded. The Dockerfile copies it deliberately (a container started
# without it had no adapters at all — see the comment in deploy/Dockerfile).

# --- runtime state that must never enter an image layer
data/
logs/
uploads/
*.db
*.db-journal
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.key
*.pem
secret.key
init_token.txt
jwt.key
cookies.txt
.env
.env.*

# --- tooling caches and environments
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/
node_modules/
webui/node_modules/
webui/.vite/
src/webui/dist/

# --- build outputs of the host (the image builds its own)
dist/
build/
dist_check/

# --- not needed to build the wheel
tests/
docs/
.github/
scripts/
plugins/
Makefile
*.md
!README.md
.codebuddy/
.git/
.gitignore
